#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552722 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ e^{u} \\\\ - 2 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- 2 t^{2} - 2 t + 1$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ e^{u} \\\\ - 4 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- 2 t^{2} - 2 t + 1\\right)=- 4 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 4 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} + 2 t - 1 \\right)} \\\\ e^{- 2 t^{2} - 2 t + 1} \\\\ 8 t^{2} + 8 t - 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(- 4 t - 2\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(- 4 t - 2\\right) \\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} + 2 t - 1 \\right)} \\\\ \\left(- 4 t - 2\\right) e^{- 2 t^{2} - 2 t + 1} \\\\ - 32 t^{3} - 48 t^{2} + 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552729 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 3 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=2 t^{3} - 3 t - 1$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$ Write the polynomial component in expanded form." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 3 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 6 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(2 t^{3} - 3 t - 1\\right)=6 t^{2} - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 6 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{2 t^{3} - 3 t - 1} \\\\ - 12 t^{3} + 18 t + 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(6 t^{2} - 3\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(6 t^{2} - 3\\right) \\cos{\\left(- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ \\left(6 - 12 t^{2}\\right) e^{2 t^{3} - 3 t - 1} \\\\ - 72 t^{5} + 144 t^{3} + 36 t^{2} - 54 t - 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552733 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - e^{u} \\\\ - 2 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- t^{2} - 2 t - 2$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - e^{u} \\\\ - 4 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- t^{2} - 2 t - 2\\right)=- 2 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ - e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 4 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(t^{2} + 2 t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ - e^{- t^{2} - 2 t - 2} \\\\ 4 t^{2} + 8 t + 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(- 2 t - 2\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(4 t + 4\\right) \\cos{\\left(t^{2} + 2 t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ 2 \\left(t + 1\\right) e^{- t^{2} - 2 t - 2} \\\\ - 8 \\left(t + 1\\right) \\left(t^{2} + 2 t + 2\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552735 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u} \\\\ - u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + 2 t + 1$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(2 e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 2 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(t^{2} + 2 t + 1\\right)=2 t + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 2 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(t^{2} + 2 t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{t^{2} + 2 t + 1} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} - 4 t - 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(2 t + 2\\right) \\cos{\\left(t^{2} + 2 t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 4 \\left(t + 1\\right) e^{t^{2} + 2 t + 1} \\\\ - 4 \\left(t + 1\\right) \\left(t^{2} + 2 t + 1\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552743 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ e^{u} \\\\ - 2 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- t^{2} + 2 t - 2$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ e^{u} \\\\ - 4 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- t^{2} + 2 t - 2\\right)=2 - 2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 4 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(t^{2} - 2 t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ e^{- t^{2} + 2 t - 2} \\\\ 4 t^{2} - 8 t + 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(2 - 2 t\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(4 - 4 t\\right) \\cos{\\left(t^{2} - 2 t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ 2 \\left(1 - t\\right) e^{- t^{2} + 2 t - 2} \\\\ - 8 \\left(t - 1\\right) \\left(t^{2} - 2 t + 2\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552753 :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-1,3,-5⟩,$ $b=⟨2,-4,4⟩,$ and $c=⟨-1,3,-5⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =8i+6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨8,6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨8,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8+3\\cdot 6+(-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552766 :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨4,-3,-1⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,-5,-2⟩,$ and $c=⟨-2,-3,-3⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-5)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =9i+j-7k \\\\  & =⟨9,1,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨4,-3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨9,1,-7⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 9+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552770 :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-2,3,-5⟩,$ $b=⟨2,-3,5⟩,$ and $c=⟨-1,1,-2⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-5)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552775 :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨4,2,-4⟩,$ $b=⟨-3,-1,-4⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,5,-4⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-4)\\cdot 5)i-((-3)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot (-4))j+((-3)\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =24i-24j-12k \\\\  & =⟨24,-24,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨4,2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨24,-24,-12⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 24+2\\cdot (-24)+(-4)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =96-48+48 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552776 :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-5,-5,1⟩,$ $b=⟨1,-1,-3⟩,$ and $c=⟨5,-3,2⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3))i-(1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-3))j+(1\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-11i-17j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-11,-17,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-5,-5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-11,-17,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-11)+(-5)\\cdot (-17)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =55+85+2 \\\\  & =142\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552782 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(2,0)$ and $(-79,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 - 3 t^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 - 3 t^{3},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 - 3 t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 9 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 9 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552800 :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 48 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\int_{1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $4x=y$ traversed between the points $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4x=y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=\\frac{y}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle\\frac{t}{4},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=48 x^{2} y^{3},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=48\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)^2t^{3} \\\\ &=48\\left(\\frac{t^2}{16}\\right)t^{3} \\\\ &=3 t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 48 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}(3 t^{5})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\int_{1}^{2}3 t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552818 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{3} - 4$ traversed between the points $(-4,0)$ and $(23,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=t^{3} - 4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t^{3} - 4,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3} - 4)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(3 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(3 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}t^{2} \\\\ &=2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552819 :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 4 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{5}\\int_{2}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2 y + 2$ traversed between the points $(6,2)$ and $(10,4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t + 2,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 2\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(2)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y^{3},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4\\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}t^{3} \\\\ &=4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}(4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\int_{2}^{4}4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552826 :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C x - 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{10}\\int_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{4}=1 - \\frac{3 y}{4}$ traversed between the points $(4,0)$ and $(-8,4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=1 - \\frac{3 y}{4}\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=4 - 3 y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=4 - 3 t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 4 - 3 t,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 - 3 t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(-3)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x - 2 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=1\\left(4 - 3 t\\right)-2t \\\\ &=4 - 5 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x - 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 5 t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 5 t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552827 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{2} + 3$ traversed between the points $(5,1)$ and $(21,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t^{2} + 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2} + 3,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2} + 3)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(4 t)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(4 t)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}t^{2} \\\\ &=2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552831 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(6,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2} + 9}{t^2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{|t|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=y^{2} e^{x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=1\\left(e^{3\\ln t}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=1\\left(t^{3}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(t^{5})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552833 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{3} + 2$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(130,4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t^{3} + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t^{3} + 2,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{3} + 2)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(6 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(6 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4\\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}(4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552841 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(4,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4 y e^{x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4 y e^{x} \\\\ &=4\\cdot t^{1}e^{2\\ln t} \\\\ &=4\\cdot t^{1}\\left(t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=4 t^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(4 t^{3})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{4}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552845 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(3,0)$ and $(-78,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 - 3 t^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 - 3 t^{3},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 - 3 t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 9 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 9 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=5 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5\\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553511 :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(0,2),$ and $(-1,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/848dfedddea9803dcf39b4624f2966b63f080920ed19b875dc691e8fe9d3b038.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553554 :question/problem "The inequality $1\\le x\\le 5$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $5$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[1,5]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,5].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553576 :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(-1,-2),(3,-2),$ and $(3,0),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e1e4386f3cc75afb63823d74daa27b517e8e47d51fa08c5c9e5bf3ca6f3ca88c.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{6}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{6},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3{y}^{2}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-2\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{6}-3{y}^{2}{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553692 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{8}=\\cos\\theta,\\quad \\frac{y}{2}=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8} & =\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(8)^{2}} & =\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64} & =\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2)^{2}} & =\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553704 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{9}=\\cos\\theta,\\quad \\frac{y}{2}=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{9} & =\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(9)^{2}} & =\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & =\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2)^{2}} & =\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553706 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{9}=\\cos\\theta,\\quad \\frac{y}{7}=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{9} & =\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(9)^{2}} & =\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & =\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{7} & =\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(7)^{2}} & =\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553716 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\cos\\theta,\\quad \\frac{y}{7}=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2)^{2}} & =\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{7} & =\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(7)^{2}} & =\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553719 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{11}=\\cos\\theta,\\quad \\frac{y}{4}=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{11} & =\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(11)^{2}} & =\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{121} & =\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4)^{2}} & =\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553730 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=x+2,$ traversed counterclockwise. Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\langle - 4 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)},x^{2} + y^{2}\\rangle,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C\\left(- 4 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=- 4 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- 8 y,\\\\\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\,x^2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C\\left(- 4 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left((2 x)-(- 8 y)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D 2 x + 8 y\\,dA\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{x + 2}2 x + 8 y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553731 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=x+2,$ traversed counterclockwise. Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\langle - 2 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)},x^{2} + y^{2}\\rangle,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C\\left(- 2 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=- 2 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- 4 y,\\\\\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\,x^2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C\\left(- 2 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left((2 x)-(- 4 y)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D 2 x + 4 y\\,dA\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{x + 2}2 x + 4 y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553734 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=x+2,$ traversed counterclockwise. Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\langle - 5 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)},x^{2} + y^{2}\\rangle,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C\\left(- 5 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=- 5 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- 10 y,\\\\\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\,x^2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C\\left(- 5 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left((2 x)-(- 10 y)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D 2 x + 10 y\\,dA\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{x + 2}2 x + 10 y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553739 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=x+2,$ traversed counterclockwise. Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\langle - 3 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)},x^{2} + y^{2}\\rangle,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C\\left(- 3 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=- 3 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=- 6 y,\\\\\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\,x^2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C\\left(- 3 y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left((2 x)-(- 6 y)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D 2 x + 6 y\\,dA\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{x + 2}2 x + 6 y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553757 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x^{2} + 4 y, \\; 2 x^{2} + 4 x y \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 y$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 x y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=4 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=4 x.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{2}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(8 x^{3}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{4}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553764 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 28 x y, \\; 12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=28 x y$ and $Q(x,y)=12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=28 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=12 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{3}}\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[12 x^{2} y + 14 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{3}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(x^{5} \\left(14 x + 12\\right)\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{7} + 2 x^{6}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553771 :question/problem "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}9\\left({y}^{2}-2x\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9\\left({y}^{2}-2x\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\left((1+2t)^{2}-2(2t)\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(4{t}^{2}+1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0)\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553772 :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}3\\left(\\sqrt{x-1}+y\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3\\left(\\sqrt{x-1}+y\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\left(\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left(\\sqrt{t}-t+2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left({[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left((\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0)\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{13}{6}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553774 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}7\\left(x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left(x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((3\\operatorname{cos}t){e}^{1-\\operatorname{sin}^{2}t}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\left(-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t\\right)& =7\\left(-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553777 :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}7\\left({x}^{2}y\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left({x}^{2}y\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\left(4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)& =7\\left(4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-(-1)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553794 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}7\\left(x+\\sqrt{3y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(0,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(0-2)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left(x+\\sqrt{3y}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((2-2t)+\\sqrt{3(3t)}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3{t}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3{[2t-{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3[(2(1)-(1)^{2}+2(1)^{3/2})-(0)]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3(3)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(9\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553809 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2 t+5,\\qquad y=t^{3}+5 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2 (-2)+5=-4+5=1 \\\\ y & =(-2)^{3}+5 (-2)=-8-10=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,-18).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=3 t^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{3 t^{2}+5}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{3 (-2)^{2}+5}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(1,-18)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{17}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny+18=\\frac{17}{2}(x-1).\n$$"}]}
